1|0@masseffect^1438500939#ctooz10:florinandrei$:Saren is one of the best villains in all entertainment I've seen so far - and I include here books and movies, not just games. I ended up sort of... well... not exactly liking him, but I kind of saw where he's coming from. Never approved of what he did, but he was an adversary you could respect. I think the game painted him in an unnecessarily negative light - via Anderson's stories. They could have just explained everything about Saren via [Sovereign's influence](/spoiler "ME1"). If Saren is as bad as Anderson says he is, then Saren [redeeming himself as one of the game finale options](/spoiler "ME1") is not very believable. Maybe Anderson remembers him in a biased way, due to what happened between them. Also, culturally, Turians seem to have a perennial stick up their ass, if you're judging them from a human perspective.
2|0@masseffect^1438544388#ctp4tnq:TheKinglyGuy$: Him redeeming himself in the end was how you could see just how far gone he was. I think he DID hate humans but wouldn't have brought a entire race of mass genociding robots to kill one species. Anderson may have been a bit biased but I could also see Saren blaming him for a mission break. I could see him not wanting a human Spectre because we are still the "new kids" pretty much and the first contact war. Saren was a great enemy, not the best villian too me, but he was definitely in the top 10. He was respectable and at least if he REALLY wanted you dead he would come down himself and try.
1|0@masseffect^1438500688#ctoowlb:florinandrei$:&gt; I think I killed.... two people in a conversation? The OP was probably talking about major characters. But yeah, even some of the minor characters show up later if you don't off them. Not all, but some. &gt; I chose to sacrifice the Council and go full blown attack on Sovereign. Out of fear, I did the same on my first play. But it's not necessary. You can save everyone. And the Asari are somewhat more friendly later in the trilogy if you save their mothership. But it doesn't really matter, your choices are your own. Keep playing the next chapters in the trilogy. It's best to do a whole playthrough for the first time knowing as little as possible about the game. You can always do a whole'nother playthrough later, with different choices. Most of us did. Playing the trilogy in a very different way later just adds to the charm. &gt; fuck Udina Aw man. Totally. It's funny how I ended up feeling respect for Saren, an adversary, while I never felt the same about Udina, an ally.
